-- Passage rg12-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('82995920-1dae-53b5-b0f3-13fc0535d6a7', 'rg12-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Clock Tower and the Sparrow$q$, $q$In the square of a busy market town there stood a clock tower that was very proud of its accuracy. Each hour it rang its bell, and each hour the merchants below adjusted their watches, and the clock tower considered this a sign of deep respect.

A sparrow built a nest in the gap behind the clock's great minute hand. The tower was offended. "You are a small, untidy creature," it said, "and you are living inside the most important instrument in this town. Everyone here depends on me. No one depends on you."

The sparrow, who was busy with twigs, said only that the gap was warm.

That autumn, a storm cracked one of the tower's gears. The clock began to run slow, losing a minute each day and then two. The merchants, who had always trusted the bell, arrived late to their stalls, closed late in the evening, and quarreled with one another about who had cheated whom.

The clock tower insisted, loudly and every hour, that it was correct.

One morning a girl named Ilse, who sold apples near the fountain, noticed that the sparrow left its nest at the same moment every day, when the first light touched the eastern roofs. She began to set her watch by the bird. Within a week her neighbors had done the same.

When the repairman finally came in the spring, he fixed the gear and asked the merchants why they had not complained sooner.

"We did not notice," said Ilse, "that we needed the clock."

The tower rang its bell at noon, exactly on time. Several people looked up. Most of them were looking at the sparrow.$q$, 'draft')
