-- Passage rg12-literary-structure-pov-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('292d4398-3385-5e97-9236-fc0ae5fd6afc', 'rg12-literary-structure-pov-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Notes from an Amateur Astronomer$q$, $q$October 3. Tonight I made what may be the most important observation in the history of our county. While the rest of my family watched television, I set up my telescope on the back porch and pointed it toward the western sky, where I found a faint, fuzzy patch of light that appears on none of my star charts. I have named it Comet Adeyemi, after myself, since I was the one who found it.

October 4. The comet is still there, in exactly the same position relative to the porch railing. This is unusual for a comet, but I believe it shows remarkable patience. My sister Funmi asked whether I had cleaned the lens. I explained, as kindly as I could, that real scientists do not have time for housekeeping.

October 6. I have written to three observatories. None has replied yet, which I take to mean they are busy verifying my discovery and do not want to raise my hopes. Funmi offered to look through the telescope herself. I told her that the comet is shy and appears only to its discoverer.

October 9. A strange development. I pointed the telescope at the moon tonight, purely for comparison, and found a faint, fuzzy patch on the moon as well, in the same corner of the view. Possibly my comet is following my telescope. This would be a first in astronomy.

October 10. Funmi borrowed a soft cloth from the kitchen this afternoon. Tonight the comet has vanished completely. I have concluded that it was frightened away by the cloth, and I have asked her not to bring kitchen supplies near the porch again.$q$, 'draft')
