  ('9c4c55c6-7b9d-5fe5-b030-883b0b1cb7f9', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The poem ends with the metronome saying, "I must have lost count. That was quick." What does this ending suggest, and why is the understated wording effective?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It hints that the metronome has finally stopped working, and the flat wording shows it has no feelings about this.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It hints that the girl plays the passage her father never mastered, and the metronome's flat excuse hides how moved it is.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It hints that the girl gives up just as her father did, and the flat wording shows the metronome expected it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"It hints that the father has returned to the room, and the flat wording keeps his arrival a secret from the girl.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It hints that the girl plays the passage her father never mastered, and the metronome's flat excuse hides how moved it is.$q$, $q$"She tries the turn again" and the sudden "oh" suggest success at the spot where her father stumbled, and the metronome covers its emotion by blaming a counting error. The interjection shows feeling, so reading the line as purely mechanical misses the irony.$q$, 'draft', '17f4d92d-1a54-5d2c-9ec9-97ba34d2b3c2')
