   $q$The flashback uses very short sentences: "She drew the coastline. She measured the depths." and later "She stopped." What is the effect of this choice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The plain, clipped sentences show that Eliza was careless and rushed through her survey work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The plain, clipped sentences make the sudden cancellation feel abrupt and the loss quietly heavy.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The plain, clipped sentences speed the story up so the reader can skip to the modern scenes.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"The plain, clipped sentences signal that the narrator doubts whether Eliza did the work at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The plain, clipped sentences make the sudden cancellation feel abrupt and the loss quietly heavy.$q$, $q$The steady rhythm of work is cut by the letter and the single sentence "She stopped," which understates her disappointment and makes it felt. The text shows careful work ("worked until her boots split"), not carelessness.$q$, 'draft', 'a240e601-b238-5151-b51a-ef6370d26827')
