  ('531f2589-8aeb-5231-b042-4cc53d22ddbb', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author places the history of Mrs. Okafor and the memos before Dmitri's arrival rather than after. How does this order affect the meaning of "It stops"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Knowing the history first, readers hear the two words as loyalty to Mrs. Okafor and quiet defiance of the office.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Knowing the history first, readers hear the two words as an admission that the driver broke rules by accident.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Knowing the history first, readers hear the two words as a rude reply that shows the driver dislikes teenagers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"Knowing the history first, readers hear the two words as a simple fact about the bus schedule and nothing more.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Knowing the history first, readers hear the two words as loyalty to Mrs. Okafor and quiet defiance of the office.$q$, $q$Because readers already know about the empty stop and the ignored memos, the brief reply carries much more than its literal meaning. Taken alone it would be a plain fact, but the earlier structure loads it with history.$q$, 'draft', 'a9123e74-bca1-54be-abb7-f001a6a34f4f')
